  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nKDDC radar observations late Saturday evening show scattered \nlight to moderate rain showers are spread across our western \nzones, courtesy of a strong upper level trough located near the \nFour Corners region as per latest water vapor satellite imagery.\nShort range high resolution guidance suggests this activity \nwill continue through the remainder of the overnight period, \nmaking little to no eastward progress as the synoptic forcing \nremains largely stationary. Daytime Sunday, a rich mid-level \nmoisture plume on the eastern periphery of the upper low \napproaching the central Rockies will support persistent rain \nshowers that will make at least some eastward headway, reaching \nnear US-283 by sunset. Nearly moist adiabatic lapse rates will \npreclude sufficient instability for any severe threat, and even \nthunder will be sparse, if existent at all. Precipitation is \nprogged to continue into the overnight period, but will \ngradually taper off through the night as the trough begins to \neject onto the central plains. Monday morning lows will range \nfrom the upper 40s/low 50s west to the upper 50s east.\n\nDaytime Monday, some scattered to isolated rain showers may \nlinger into the morning, however these should dissipate by \nearly afternoon as DNVA in the wake of the ejecting upper wave \noverspreads southwest KS. Precipitation totals for this event \nare expected to be in the 1- 1.5\" range along and west of US-83,\nthe 0.25-1\" range between US-83 and US-183, and just a few \nhundredths to a perhaps a tenth east of US-183. Through the \nremainder of Monday, mostly cloudy skies and downsloping south-\nsouthwest winds will result in afternoon highs in the low 70s. \nShort range ensembles also hint at another vorticity max moving \nnortheastward from the southern plains towards our area, which \nmay spark some isolated showers/thunderstorms across our eastern\nzones. NBM paints some chance (25-54%) to likely (55-74%) pops \nin response that persist into the overnight period.\n\nTuesday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nsuggest southwesterly mid-level flow across the central Rockies\nwill be maintained, fostering unseasonably warm afternoon \ntemperatures each day with highs in the 70s to low 80s. Some \nprecipitation chances will exist near the end of the forecast \nperiod, but these are highly uncertain.",
